## PR Checklist

- [x] The commit message follows the guidelines.
- [x] Regression tests have been added.
- [ ] Docs have been added / updated.

## PR Type

- [x] Other: Performance

## What is the current behavior?

Each `[value]` / `[ngValue]` update and option destruction immediately calls `SelectMultipleControlValueAccessor.writeValue()`. That method scans the registered options and writes every option's `selected` property.

Creating a batch of N options therefore produces N(N+1)/2 selection writes, even with an empty selection. For 2,000 options this is 2,001,000 writes. Refreshing object-valued options also repeats the reconciliation for every changed binding.

Related: #41330.

## What is the new behavior?

Coalesce option-triggered selection updates into one `afterNextRender` write callback, following `SelectControlValueAccessor`'s existing scheduling. Read the current model when the callback runs. Direct `FormControl` writes remain synchronous, and deferred work is skipped after the select or application injector is destroyed.

Add six regression tests for both `[value]` and `[ngValue]`: batch addition, batch removal, and select destruction. All six fail on the original implementation and pass with the change.

## Production measurements

Measured a native multi-select demo using Angular 22.2.3 with only this patch applied: AOT compilation, Angular linker, minification, `ngDevMode=false`, `ngJitMode=false`; Chrome 155 on Linux, without CPU throttling.

Medians of 11 measured pairs after two warmup pairs; baseline/patched execution order alternates. The interval starts at a signal update and ends at `ApplicationRef.whenStable()`, including Angular scheduling, native DOM updates, and `afterNextRender`. Input arrays are prepared before timing; startup, network and paint are outside the interval.

With 2,000 options and the last item selected:

| Action | Baseline | Patched |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Add options | 180.6 ms | 22.2 ms |
| Refresh objects with the same IDs | 220.4 ms | 43.4 ms |
| Remove options | 98.4 ms | 13.2 ms |

With an empty selection, adding 2,000 options takes 142.1 ms versus 22.8 ms. Selection correctness is asserted after every action. Separate checks cover custom `compareWith`, synchronous model writes, reordering, refreshed objects, removal of the selected option and native change events.

<details>
<summary>Minimal reproduction component</summary>

Use an AOT production build, compare the base branch with this change, and repeat after warmup. The first click creates options; subsequent clicks refresh their objects while retaining DOM nodes through `track item.id`.

```ts
import {ApplicationRef, Component, inject, signal} from '@angular/core';
import {FormControl, ReactiveFormsModule} from '@angular/forms';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  imports: [ReactiveFormsModule],
  template: `
    <button (click)="load()">Load / refresh 2,000 options</button>
    <select multiple [formControl]="control" [compareWith]="compareById">
      @for (item of items(); track item.id) {
        <option [ngValue]="item">{{ item.id }}</option>
      }
    </select>
  `,
})
export class App {
  readonly items = signal<{id: number}[]>([]);
  readonly control = new FormControl([{id: 1999}], {nonNullable: true});
  readonly compareById = (a: {id: number}, b: {id: number}) => a?.id === b?.id;
  private readonly app = inject(ApplicationRef);

  async load() {
    const next = Array.from({length: 2000}, (_, id) => ({id}));
    const start = performance.now();
    this.items.set(next);
    await this.app.whenStable();
    console.log(performance.now() - start);
  }
}
```

</details>

## Does this PR introduce a breaking change?

- [ ] Yes
- [x] No

## Validation

- `//packages/forms/test:test`: 1,162 specs, 0 failures, including the six new regression tests. All six new tests fail on the base implementation.
- `//packages/forms:forms_api`: passed.
- Reactive and template-driven Forms symbol-golden tests: passed.
- Changed-file TSLint and Angular formatting checks: passed.
- Chromium and Firefox test targets could not start locally: the test runner fails in `os.networkInterfaces()` with `uv_interface_addresses` / `ERR_SYSTEM_ERROR` before executing specs. The production Chrome benchmark above was run separately.
